# Opening a JES scene
@scenario tutorial_lesson_17
@include /definitions/characters.vns
@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[bg room]
[char ginger global on]
[set ui.dialogueFadeMs 160]
[stage clear]
[show ginger beside_code normal]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/17_vns_jes_integration.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt open_scene]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: A VNS script can open a JES scene, then resume at a specified label. The example below opens a panel with two keyboard handlers.
ginger: Choose {b}Open the example scene{/b}. Press Enter there. Try it again with Esc. Both inputs should return to the review question.
[set tutorial_source scripts/scenes/panel.jes]
[set tutorial_excerpt input]
ginger: These two handlers live in {b}scripts/scenes/panel.jes{/b}. Either key returns to the label supplied by the VNS command.
ginger: Edit the panel's title in the JES file, then reopen it. The title should change while the return destination stays the same.
ginger: There are two files and two entry points to track. The VNS command chooses a JES file and a VNS return label. The JES file owns the scene entities and the inputs that leave it.
ginger: Trace Enter and Esc separately. Both handlers request a return, so either input should take you back to check in the originating lesson.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Open the example scene -> visit
> Trace the VNS to JES return -> practice
> Review question -> check
> Back to topics -> return_hub
# region open_scene
@label visit
[jes push scripts/scenes/panel.jes label check]
# endregion
@label check
ginger: Where do you edit the example panel?
> In the .jes scene that owns it. -> correct
> In the speaker declaration. -> retry
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label retry
[show ginger guide confused]
ginger: A speaker declaration names a dialogue participant. The panel is made of entities in a separate scene.
[jump check]
@label correct
[show ginger guide normal]
ginger: Edit the .jes scene that owns the entities. Test the visual change and the return input separately.
> Back to topics -> return_hub
> Replay example -> start
@label return_hub
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
[if !tutorial_done_17]
[flag tutorial_done_17]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[stage clear]
[goto TutorialHub:topics_page_5]
[end]

@label practice
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_source scripts/scenes/panel.jes]
[set tutorial_excerpt input]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: Start at the VNS line that pushes the panel. Underline the script path and the word after label. A valid scene path can open the right panel even when the return target is wrong.
ginger: In the JES file, find the title entity and the two input handlers. Edit its title, open the panel and return with Enter. Repeat the trip and use Esc.
ginger: If the panel title changed but the return destination did not, that is the expected split of responsibility. To change where VNS resumes, edit the label on the push command.
ginger: If one key returns and the other does nothing, keep the VNS target fixed and compare the two JES bindings. One successful return is evidence that the caller's target can work.
@label practice_question
ginger: Enter returns correctly and Esc does nothing on the same panel. Which file do you inspect first?
> The JES file that binds the two keys. -> practice_result
> The VNS file that names their common return target. -> practice_hint
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label practice_hint
ginger: Both inputs share the VNS destination. A difference between the keys points first to how the scene binds them.
[jump practice_question]
@label practice_result
ginger: One handler already reaches the right destination. Check the other key name and action in the scene before changing the caller.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Review question -> check
> Try the exercise again -> practice
> Back to topics -> return_hub
